    user: "Unsa man ang climate zones sa Pilipinas ug giunsa pag-classify?",
    assistant: "Maayo kaayo nga pangutana! Ang Pilipinas may diverse nga climate patterns tungod sa atong geography — archipelago nga naa sa tropics, may kabukiran, ug surrounded sa kadagatan. Atong hisgutan ang **Modified Coronas Classification** nga gigamit sa PAGASA.\n\n**Ang Climate Classification System sa Pilipinas:**\n\nAng Pilipinas naggamit sa **Modified Coronas Classification** (gi-develop ni Jose Coronas niadtong 1920). Kini base sa **monthly rainfall distribution** — kung kanus-a ang wet ug dry months sa usa ka lugar.\n\n**4 ka Climate Types:**\n\n**Type I: Two Pronounced Seasons**\n- **Dry season:** Nobyembre hangtod Abril\n- **Wet season:** Mayo hangtod Oktubre\n- Maximum rain months: Hulyo-Septiyembre\n- Pinaka-distinct nga wet ug dry seasons\n- **Areas:** Western Luzon (Manila, Bataan, Zambales, Ilocos), Western Visayas (Iloilo, Bacolod), Palawan\n- Ang Habagat (SW monsoon) ang nagdala sa wet season\n\n**Type II: No Dry Season, Very Pronounced Maximum Rainfall**\n- Walay dry season — ulan throughout the year\n- Pinaka-rainy months: Nobyembre hangtod Enero\n- Maximum rainfall sa winter months\n- **Areas:** Eastern Luzon (Bicol, Aurora, Quezon), Eastern Visayas (Leyte, Samar), Eastern Mindanao (Surigao, Davao Oriental)\n- Ang Amihan (NE monsoon) nagdala og moisture gikan sa Pacific\n\n**Type III: Not Very Pronounced Dry and Wet Seasons**\n- May dry season pero short lang (1-3 months)\n- Not very pronounced ang seasons\n- **Areas:** Central Luzon, parts sa Western Visayas, Northern Mindanao, Cebu, Bohol\n- Transition zone tali sa Type I ug Type II\n\n**Type IV: Rainfall More or Less Evenly Distributed Throughout the Year**\n- Walay distinct dry o wet season\n- Rainfall distributed throughout the year\n- **Areas:** Eastern Mindanao (Agusan, Davao del Sur), Sulu Archipelago, parts sa Central Visayas\n- Ang ITCZ ug localized convection ang nagdala sa rainfall\n\n**Mapa sa Climate Types:**\n\nKung tan-awon nimo ang climate map sa Pilipinas:\n- **Western side** (facing South China Sea): kasagaran Type I (distinct dry/wet)\n- **Eastern side** (facing Pacific): kasagaran Type II (walay dry season)\n- **Central areas:** Type III o Type IV (transition)\n- **Southern Mindanao:** Type IV (even rainfall)\n\n**Ngano nga Lain-lain ang Climate sa Pilipinas?**\n\n**1. Geographic Position:**\n- Ang Pilipinas archipelago — 7,641 ka islands\n- Ang western side na-expose sa South China Sea ug Amihan (dry)\n- Ang eastern side na-expose sa Pacific Ocean ug Amihan (moist)\n\n**2. Monsoons:**\n- Amihan: nagdala og dry air sa west, rain sa east\n- Habagat: nagdala og rain sa west\n\n**3. Mountain Ranges:**\n- Ang Sierra Madre, Cordillera, ug Zambales Mountains nag-block sa rain\n- **Rain shadow effect:** Ang western side sa mountains mas uga\n- Example: Baguio (eastern side sa Zambales) may more rain kaysa Ilocos (western side)\n\n**4. ITCZ:**\n- Ang seasonal migration sa ITCZ nagdala og rainfall sa lain-laing areas\n\n**5. Typhoons:**\n- Ang typhoons kasagaran mo-landfall sa eastern Pilipinas\n- Nag-contribute sa heavy rainfall sa Type II areas\n\n**Epekto sa Climate Types sa Agriculture:**\n\n**Type I Areas:**\n- Rice planting: sa wet season (Mayo-Oktubre)\n- Dry season crops: tobacco, garlic, onions\n- Irrigation importante sa dry season\n\n**Type II Areas:**\n- Rice pwede tibuok tuig (kung may irrigation)\n- Coconut, abaca thrive sa year-round rain\n- Typhoon damage risk mas taas\n\n**Type III Areas:**\n- Flexible planting schedules\n- Diverse crops\n- Sugarcane (Negros), corn\n\n**Type IV Areas:**\n- Year-round agriculture possible\n- Rice, corn, root crops\n- Less risk sa drought\n\n**Climate Zones sa Global Context:**\n\n**Köppen Climate Classification (Global):**\nAng Pilipinas mostly classified isip:\n- **Af (Tropical Rainforest):** Eastern areas (Type II, IV)\n- **Am (Tropical Monsoon):** Central areas (Type III)\n- **Aw (Tropical Savanna):** Western areas (Type I)\n\n**Altitude ug Microclimates:**\n- Ang taas nga areas (Baguio, Tagaytay) may **subtropical highland climate**\n- Temperature: 15-25°C (mas bugnaw kaysa lowlands)\n- Baguio: 1,500 m elevation, average temp 18°C\n- Ang temperature mo-kunhod og 6.5°C kada 1,000 m (environmental lapse rate)\n\n**Importance sa Climate Classification:**\n\n**1. Agriculture:**\n- PAGASA nag-issue og agrometeorological advisories\n- Pagplano sa planting schedules\n- Crop selection base sa climate type\n\n**2. Water Resources:**\n- Dam operations depende sa rainfall patterns\n- Water supply planning\n\n**3. Disaster Preparedness:**\n- Type I: Drought preparedness sa dry season\n- Type II: Typhoon ug flood preparedness\n\n**4. Tourism:**\n- Dry season (Nov-Apr): beach destinations (Boracay, Palawan)\n- Wet season: surfing (Siargao peak season)\n\n**5. Health:**\n- Wet season: dengue, leptospirosis\n- Dry season: heat stroke, water shortage\n\n**Climate Variability:**\n\n**El Niño Effects:**\n- Type I areas: severe drought, water crisis\n- Type II areas: reduced rainfall\n- Crop failures, water rationing\n\n**La Niña Effects:**\n- Type I areas: more rain than usual\n- Type II areas: extreme rainfall, flooding\n- Landslides sa mountainous areas\n\n**PAGASA Climate Monitoring:**\n- 60+ weather stations sa tibuok nasud\n- Automatic weather stations (AWS)\n- Satellite data\n- Climate models (downscaling global models)\n- Seasonal climate outlooks (matag 3 months)\n\n**Interesting Facts:**\n\n**1. Wettest Place:**\n- **Baguio City** — average annual rainfall: 4,500 mm\n- Record: 2,287 mm sa usa ka bulan (Hulyo 1927!)\n\n**2. Driest Place:**\n- **General Santos City** — average annual rainfall: 1,000 mm\n- Type I climate, distinct dry season\n\n**3. Coldest Place:**\n- **Baguio** — record low: 6.3°C (January 18, 1961)\n\n**4. Hottest Place:**\n- **Tuguegarao, Cagayan** — record high: 42.2°C (May 11, 1969)\n\n**5. Most Typhoon-Prone:**\n- **Eastern Visayas ug Bicol** — first landfall areas\n\n**Pangutana para nimo:** Ngano nga ang Cebu may mas gamay nga rainfall kaysa sa Leyte, bisan parehas sila sa Central Visayas? Unsa ang papel sa mountain ranges sa pag-create og rain shadow? Ug kung magbag-o ang climate tungod sa global warming, unsa ang mahitabo sa atong climate classification — basin mabag-o ang Type I ngadto sa Type II, o vice versa?"
